.TH MERCURY 1 "2026" "Mercury 1.9.19" "Mercury Manual"
.SH NAME
mercury \- HF radio modem with ARQ and broadcast data links
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B mercury
.RB [ \-i
.IR device ]
.RB [ \-o
.IR device ]
.RB [ \-x
.IR sound_system ]
.RB [ \-m
.IR mode_index ]
.RB [ \-p
.IR port ]
.RB [ \-b
.IR port ]
.RB [ \-R
.IR model ]
.RB [ \-A
.IR address ]
.RB [ \-S ]
.RB [ \-G ]
.RB [ options ]
.br
.B mercury
.RB [ \-l " | " \-z " | " \-K " | " \-Q " | " \-V " | " \-h ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B mercury
is the modem component of the HERMES (High-Frequency Emergency and Rural
Multimedia Exchange System) project.
It provides ARQ (Automatic Repeat reQuest) and broadcast data links over
HF radio using FreeDV OFDM waveforms with LDPC forward error correction.
.PP
The ARQ link is a half-duplex, receiver-driven erasure-coded
\(lqcarousel\(rq: the application's bytes are cut into Reed-Solomon blocks,
the receiver polls for what each block still needs, and any piece of a block
replaces a lost one.
Each direction runs on one of seven rungs \(em the 32-tone MFSK floor,
DATAC15, DATAC4, DATAC3, DATAC1, DATAC17 and QAM16C2 \(em starting on the
rung the connect's SNR supports and moving on measured delivery.
The session bandwidth caps the rungs: BW500 uses MFSK through DATAC3.
.PP
Since the carousel-only build the connect frames carry a protocol version:
Mercury 1.9.x stations do not connect to this version in either direction.
.PP
Mercury exposes a VARA-compatible TNC (Terminal Node Controller) TCP
interface, allowing existing amateur radio software such as UUCP, Winlink,
and Pat to connect without modification.
.SH OPTIONS
.SS "Audio options"
.TP
.BI \-i " device"
Radio capture (input) device identifier.
For ALSA, use a device string such as \(lqplughw:0,0\(rq.
For PulseAudio, WASAPI, or DirectSound, omit this option to use the system default.
.TP
.BI \-o " device"
Radio playback (output) device identifier.
Same format as
.BR \-i .
.TP
.BI \-x " sound_system"
Audio subsystem to use.
Valid values:
.BR alsa ,
.BR pulse ,
.BR dsound ,
.BR wasapi ,
.BR oss ,
.BR coreaudio ,
.BR aaudio ,
.BR shm ,
.BR rtp ,
.BR null ,
.BR fifo ,
or
.BR sock .
Default is
.B alsa
on Linux and
.B dsound
on Windows.
.B null
is a developer/test backend that feeds RX silence and discards TX audio.
.B fifo
is a developer/test backend using raw signed 32-bit little-endian PCM at 8 kHz via
.B \-i
and
.B \-o
paths.
.B sock
is a framed, virtual-clock lockstep transport for tests
(MERCURY_AUDIO_SOCK=\fIpath\fR).
.B rtp
exchanges the radio signal with hermes-radio-daemon as RTP multicast:
.B \-i
.IR "rx group" [, iface ]
(default 239.255.72.1,lo),
.B \-o
.I "tx group"
(default 239.255.72.2); PTT travels in the TX stream.
.TP
.BI \-k " channel"
Capture input channel layout:
.BR left ,
.BR right ,
or
.BR stereo .
Default is
.BR left .
.TP
.B \-z
List all available sound cards for the selected audio subsystem and exit.
.SS "Modem options"
.TP
.BI \-m " mode_index"
Startup payload mode index (see
.BR \-l ).
Used for broadcast, the test modes, and what the payload decoder listens on
while no ARQ session is up; it does not affect an ARQ session.
Default is 1 (DATAC3).
.TP
.BI \-s " mode_index"
Legacy alias for
.BR \-m .
.TP
.BI \-f " level"
FreeDV modem verbosity level, 0 to 3.
Default is 0.
.TP
.BI \-H " level"
Hamlib radio log level, 0 to 6. 0=NONE, 1=BUG, 2=ERR, 3=WARN, 4=VERBOSE, 5=TRACE, 6=CACHE.
Default is 0.
.TP
.B \-l
List all available modulation/coding modes with their parameters and exit.
.TP
.B \-t
Test TX (transmit) mode.
.TP
.B \-r
Test RX (receive) mode.
.SS "Network options"
.TP
.BI \-p " port"
ARQ TCP base port.
The control port is
.IR port ,
and the data port is
.IR port +1.
Default is
.BR 8300 .
.TP
.BI \-b " port"
Broadcast TCP port.
Default is
.BR 8100 .
.SS "UI options"
.TP
.B \-G
Enable UI communication (WebSocket server for
.BR mercury-qt ).
Off by default.
.TP
.BI \-U " port"
UI WebSocket port.
Default is
.BR 10000 .
Requires
.BR \-G .
.TP
.B \-T
Use WSS (WebSocket Secure / TLS) for UI communication instead of plain WS.
Requires
.BR \-G ,
and a build with OpenSSL, which is used wherever the system provides it; a
build without it refuses this option at startup and says so.
The certificate and key default to
.I /etc/ssl/certs/hermes.radio.crt
and
.IR /etc/ssl/private/hermes.radio.key ,
and are overridden with the
.B ui_tls_cert
and
.B ui_tls_key
keys in the configuration file \(em needed to run the UI as a normal user, or
on Windows and macOS where those paths do not exist.
.TP
.B \-W
Disable waterfall/spectrum data sent to the UI (saves CPU).
.SS "PTT control options"
.TP
.BI \-P " method"
Select the PTT method:
.BR none ,
.BR hamlib ,
.BR serial ,
.BR cm108 ,
or
.BR hermes_shm .
.TP
.BI \-R " model"
Set Hamlib radio model (numeric ID) and select the Hamlib PTT method.
Use
.B \-K
to list available models.
.TP
.BI \-A " device"
Set the selected PTT method's serial device or Hamlib ip:port endpoint (e.g.,
.IR /dev/ttyUSB0 ,
.IR COM3 ,
or
.IR localhost:4532 ).
.TP
.B \-S
Select the HERMES shared-memory PTT method.
Available only on Linux builds.
.TP
.B \-K
List Hamlib supported radio models and exit.
.TP
.B \-Q
Open the configured PTT backend, key it for one second, release it, and exit
without starting the modem or audio devices. This can be combined with
.BR \-C ,
.BR \-P ,
.BR \-A ,
and the serial settings in
.BR mercury.ini .
.SS "Logging options"
.TP
.BI \-L " path"
Write log to file (TIMING level and above).
In verbose mode, log level is DEBUG.
On Windows, without
.BR \-L ,
the log goes to
.I logs\emercury_engine.log
beside
.IR mercury.exe ,
as the GUI\(aqs does.
.TP
.B \-J
Use JSONL format for the log file (requires
.BR \-L ).
.TP
.B \-v
Verbose mode.
Enables debug-level logging.
.TP
.B \-d
Trace mode.
Logs the raw bytes of every decoded connect frame (TRACE level); implies
.BR \-v .
.SS "System options"
.TP
.BI \-c " cpu"
Pin the process to CPU number
.IR cpu .
Use \-1 to disable CPU selection (default).
Linux only.
.TP
.BI \-C " path"
Path to init configuration file (INI format).
Without it, Mercury looks for
.B mercury.ini
in the places listed under
.B CONFIGURATION FILE
below.
.TP
.B \-V
Print version information and exit.
.TP
.B \-h
Print usage help and exit.
.SH CONFIGURATION FILE
Mercury reads an INI-format configuration file at startup.
Command-line arguments take priority over file values.
.PP
With
.BR \-C ,
that file is used.
Otherwise Mercury takes the first of these that exists:
.RS
.TP
.B mercury.ini
in the current working directory;
.TP
.B $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/mercury/mercury.ini
or, when XDG_CONFIG_HOME is not set,
.BR ~/.config/mercury/mercury.ini ;
.TP
.B /etc/mercury/mercury.ini
.RE
.PP
On Windows only the working directory is searched.
.PP
If the file cannot be read in full \(em it does not parse, or a setting has
an invalid value \(em Mercury prints why and exits without starting the modem
or testing PTT (\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-V\fR, \fB\-l\fR, \fB\-z\fR and
\fB\-K\fR still work).
Running on part of a configuration could open the wrong sound device or key
the wrong radio.
When there is no configuration file (none found, or the one named with
.B \-C
does not exist), Mercury runs on its built-in defaults and the command line,
and says so, naming the sound system and devices it opens.
.PP
.B mercury.ini.example
lists every setting with its default.
Packages install it as
.BR /usr/share/doc/mercury/mercury.ini.example ;
copy it to one of the places above and edit it for the radio.
.PP
General settings are under
.BR [main] ;
PTT settings are under
.BR [ptt] ;
the ARQ, audio, TNC, channel-busy and message-store settings under
.BR [arq] ,
.BR [audio] ,
.BR [tnc] ,
.B [channel]
and
.BR [store] .
The principal recognized keys are:
.PP
.TS
l l l.
Key	Default	Description
_
ui_enabled	false	Enable WebSocket UI server
ui_port	10000	WebSocket port
ui_protocol	ws	ws (plain) or wss (TLS)
ui_tls_cert	/etc/ssl/...	PEM certificate for wss
ui_tls_key	/etc/ssl/...	PEM private key for wss
waterfall_enabled	true	Send waterfall/spectrum data to UI
input_device	(empty)	Audio capture device
output_device	(empty)	Audio playback device
capture_channel	left	left, right, or stereo
sound_system	auto	Audio subsystem
arq_tcp_base_port	8300	ARQ TCP base port
broadcast_tcp_port	8100	Broadcast TCP port
verbose	false	Enable debug logging
trace	false	Log raw decoded connect-frame bytes (TRACE)
freedv_verbosity	0	FreeDV modem verbosity (0\-3)
ptt:method	none	none, hamlib, serial, cm108, or hermes_shm
ptt:line	rts	rts, dtr, or both (serial method)
ptt:invert	none	none, rts, dtr, or both (serial method)
ptt:cm108_gpio	3	CM108 GPIO pin 1..4
ptt:device	(empty)	Serial device or Hamlib endpoint
ptt:hamlib_model	\-1	Hamlib model ID
ptt:hamlib_serial_speed	0	Serial baud rate (0=Hamlib default)
ptt:hamlib_log_level	0	Hamlib debug level (0\-6)
arq:disconnect_drain_timeout_s	45	Cap on draining after DISCONNECT
arq:retry_stagger_ms	2000	Retry offset between the two peers
audio:tx_gain_db	0.0	Modulator gain
audio:tx_delay_ms	10	Delay from PTT on to audio
channel:busy_detect	false	Channel-busy (BUSY ON/OFF) detector
.TE
.PP
Keys that older versions read and this one ignores (the stop-and-wait ARQ's
.BR no_progress_timeout_s ,
.BR data_retry_slots ,
.BR keepalive_interval_s
and the like, and the guards
.B channel_guard_ms
and
.BR iss_post_ack_guard_ms )
are reported at startup with a warning; delete them from the file.
.PP
The
.B ptt:hamlib_serial_speed
setting overrides the compiled-in hamlib default baud rate for the
selected radio model.
It must match the CAT Rate configured on the radio.
Common values are 4800, 9600, 19200, 38400, and 115200.
.PP
The legacy main-section keys
.BR radio_model ,
.BR radio_device ,
.BR radio_serial_speed ,
and
.B hamlib_log_level
remain accepted and are mapped to the equivalent PTT configuration.
.SH TNC INTERFACE
Mercury provides a VARA-compatible TCP TNC interface for ARQ operations.
The control port accepts text commands terminated by a carriage return,
and sends asynchronous status responses.
The data port carries the raw byte stream.
.SS "Commands (control port)"
.TP
.BI "MYCALL " "callsign " "[secondary1 secondary2 ...]"
Set the local station callsign (up to 15 characters).
Additional space-separated tokens register secondary callsigns (up to 4);
Mercury will accept incoming connections addressed to any of them.
Sending a new MYCALL replaces the primary callsign and clears all
previously registered secondaries.
.TP
.BI "LISTEN " "ON|OFF|CQ"
Enable or disable listening for incoming connections.
.B LISTEN CQ
is treated as
.BR "LISTEN ON" .
.TP
.BI "PUBLIC " "ON|OFF"
Enable or disable public mode (accept connections from any station).
.TP
.BI "CONNECT " "mycall " destcall
Initiate an ARQ connection from
.I mycall
to
.IR destcall .
.TP
.B DISCONNECT
Disconnect the current ARQ session gracefully.
The air-side teardown proceeds in the background.
.TP
.B ABORT
Disconnect immediately without air-side teardown.
All buffers are flushed; the remote peer will time out.
.TP
.BI "BW" bandwidth
Set the channel bandwidth in Hz.
Valid values:
.B 500
(narrow: MFSK, DATAC15, DATAC4 and DATAC3),
.B 2300
(full: every rung), or
.B 2750
(tactical: for now the same rungs as 2300).
Default is 2300.
A session runs at the lower of the two stations' values, and a change applies
to the next session.
.TP
.BI "RETRIES " count
Set how many times a CALL or an ACCEPT is repeated before the connect gives
up (0 restores the default).
.TP
.BI "CALLINT " seconds
Set the interval between CALL/ACCEPT repeats (0 restores the default, 8 s;
at least 4).
.TP
.BI "MODE " index
Set the idle (listen and broadcast) payload mode, in the index space of
.BR \-l ;
refused during a session.
.B MODE
alone reports it.
.TP
.B MONITOR ON|OFF
Passive monitor: report every frame decoded on the air as a
.B MONITOR
line, without answering or keying.
.TP
.B BUFFER
Query the number of bytes pending in the transmit buffer.
.TP
.B SN
Query the current signal-to-noise ratio.
.TP
.B BITRATE
Query the current bitrate.
.TP
.BI "TUNE " -dB
Key the transmitter and hold a steady 1000 Hz tone at the given level in dBFS,
so an antenna tuner can find a match (for example
.BR "TUNE -15" ).
Accepted levels are \-60 to 0 dBFS.
The level is absolute at the sound card: the modulator TX gain is deliberately
not applied, so
.B TUNE -15
really is \-15 dBFS.
Re-issuing while tuning changes the level and restarts the safety timer.
Refused while a link is connected or a burst is in flight.
.IP
The carrier
.B always
stops by itself after 60 seconds, even if the host never sends
.BR "TUNE OFF" ,
crashes, or drops the control socket \(em nothing else bounds a keyed carrier.
.TP
.B TUNE ?
Query the current tuning drive level.
.TP
.B TUNE OFF
Stop the tuning carrier and unkey.
.TP
.BI "CQFRAME " "callsign " bandwidth
Transmit a CQ broadcast frame.
.I bandwidth
accepts the same values as
.BR BW .
.TP
.B VERSION
Return modem identification string.
Accepted for VARA compatibility.
.TP
.B COMPRESSION ON|OFF
Accepted for VARA compatibility; no-op.
.TP
.B CHAT ON|OFF
.B CHAT ON
implies
.BR "LISTEN ON" ;
.B CHAT OFF
is a no-op.
Accepted for VARA compatibility.
.TP
.B P2P
Accepted for VARA compatibility; no-op.
.TP
.B IGNOREKISSDCD
Accepted for VARA compatibility; no-op.
.SS "Responses (control port)"
.TP
.B OK
Command accepted.
.TP
.B WRONG
Command rejected or malformed.
.TP
.BI "CONNECTED " "source " "dest " bandwidth
ARQ link established between
.I source
and
.IR dest .
.TP
.B DISCONNECTED
ARQ link terminated.
.TP
.B PENDING
An incoming connect request is being answered (also: an outgoing CQ frame
started).
.TP
.B CANCELPENDING
That incoming connect did not complete (also: the outgoing CQ frame ended).
.TP
.B BUSY ON\fR, \fBBUSY OFF
The channel-busy detector's transitions (when enabled).
.TP
.B IAMALIVE
Heartbeat, sent periodically while idle.
.TP
.BI "REGISTERED " callsign
The callsign set with MYCALL is registered.
.TP
.BI "BUFFER " bytes
Number of bytes remaining in the transmit buffer.
Sent periodically and in response to BUFFER queries.
.TP
.BI "SN " snr
Current signal-to-noise ratio in dB (e.g., \(lqSN 12.5\(rq).
.TP
.BI "BITRATE (" code ") " bps " BPS"
Current bitrate in bits per second, and a code for the mode on the air:
1 DATAC1, 3 DATAC3, 4 DATAC4, 15 DATAC15, 17 DATAC17, 25 QAM16C2, 100 MFSK.
.TP
.BI "CQFRAME " callsign " " bandwidth
Received a CQ broadcast from
.IR callsign .
.SH MODES
.B mercury \-l
lists the modes by index.
Payload is the bytes a frame carries; bandwidth is the occupied carrier span.
.PP
.TS
l l l l l.
Mode	Index	Payload	Bandwidth	Use
_
DATAC1	0	510 B	1687 Hz	ARQ rung 4
DATAC3	1	126 B	562 Hz	ARQ rung 3; default \-m
DATAC0	2	14 B	562 Hz	broadcast and tests only
DATAC4	3	54 B	250 Hz	ARQ rung 2
DATAC13	4	14 B	187 Hz	broadcast and monitor
DATAC14	5	3 B	222 Hz	broadcast and tests only
FSK_LDPC	6	30 B	\(em	experimental, tests only
DATAC15	7	30 B	187 Hz	ARQ rung 1
DATAC16	8	14 B	187 Hz	ARQ control
DATAC17	9	1180 B	2062 Hz	ARQ rung 5
QAM16C2	10	1213 B	2062 Hz	ARQ rung 6
MFSK	11	98 B	1000 Hz	ARQ rung 0 (floor), CALLs and patterns there
.TE
.PP
In an ARQ session, control frames go on DATAC16 (on MFSK, or as tone
patterns, when a station hears the other below DATAC16), and each direction's
payload runs on rungs 0 to 6 \(em up to rung 3 in a BW500 session.
DATAC15, DATAC16, DATAC17, QAM16C2 and MFSK are Mercury's own modes.
See docs/MODES.md for measured per-mode performance and docs/CAROUSEL-ARQ.md
for the data link.
.SH DEFAULT PORTS
.PP
.TS
l l l.
Service	Port	Protocol
_
ARQ control	8300	TCP
ARQ data	8301	TCP
Broadcast	8100	TCP
UI (WebSocket)	10000	TCP
.TE
.SH EXAMPLES
Start mercury with default settings (ALSA, DATAC3, port 8300):
.PP
.RS
.nf
mercury \-i plughw:1,0 \-o plughw:1,0
.fi
.RE
.PP
Start with verbose logging to a JSONL file:
.PP
.RS
.nf
mercury \-i plughw:1,0 \-o plughw:1,0 \-v \-L /var/log/mercury.jsonl \-J
.fi
.RE
.PP
Start with Hamlib radio control (rigctld on localhost):
.PP
.RS
.nf
mercury \-i plughw:1,0 \-o plughw:1,0 \-R 2 \-A localhost:4532
.fi
.RE
.PP
Start with Hamlib radio control on a serial port (Yaesu FT-710 on Windows,
default DirectSound devices):
.PP
.RS
.nf
mercury \-x dsound \-R 1049 \-A COM3
.fi
.RE
.PP
Start with serial RTS PTT (for example DigiRig on Windows):
.PP
.RS
.nf
mercury \-x dsound \-P serial \-A COM7
.fi
.RE
.PP
Start with HERMES shared memory PTT control:
.PP
.RS
.nf
mercury \-i plughw:1,0 \-o plughw:1,0 \-S
.fi
.RE
.PP
Start with PulseAudio, DATAC4 as the listen/broadcast mode, and the UI over WSS:
.PP
.RS
.nf
mercury \-x pulse \-m 3 \-G \-T
.fi
.RE
.PP
Use a custom configuration file:
.PP
.RS
.nf
mercury \-C /etc/mercury/my_config.ini
.fi
.RE
.PP
List available modulation modes:
.PP
.RS
.nf
mercury \-l
.fi
.RE
.PP
List available sound cards:
.PP
.RS
.nf
mercury \-z
.fi
.RE
.PP
List supported Hamlib radio models:
.PP
.RS
.nf
mercury \-K
.fi
.RE
.SH SIGNALS
.TP
.B SIGINT\fR, \fBSIGTERM
Graceful shutdown.
Mercury releases audio devices, closes TCP connections, and shuts down
the radio control interface before exiting.
.SH FILES
.TP
.B ./mercury.ini
.TQ
